  "response": "Adjudication begins not with the structure but with the question of who is entitled to ask. The bridge deck collapsed during the placement of wet concrete, and that failure led the Occupational Safety and Health Administration to open an investigation into the collapse. The National Transportation Safety Board holds sole statutory jurisdiction over bridge construction collapses, a position that cannot be squared with the opening of a workplace-safety inquiry into the very same failure, and the unresolved conflict governs which records and witnesses the investigators can compel.\n\nThe technical question is narrowed by the loading condition obtaining at the moment of failure. Freshly placed concrete transfers its full weight to the temporary falsework supporting the deck, and that transfer of load caused the falsework to fail while the placed concrete was still in a plastic state. Cast-in-place concrete, by contrast, attains its full design compressive strength within 72 hours of placement. The competing hypotheses therefore concern the adequacy of the temporary works as designed and their fidelity as actually erected.\n\nThe allocation of responsibility for those temporary works follows the contract rather than intuition. The contractor was responsible for designing the temporary falsework for the deck pour, whereas the engineer of record reviewed the falsework submittals for conformance with the contract documents. A deficiency in shoring is accordingly not, by itself, a defect in the permanent design, and the forensic task is to locate the deficiency with enough precision to assign it to one party or the other.\n\nACI 347 provides recommended practice for the design of concrete formwork and shoring. The inquiry then moved to the site itself. Forensic engineers compared the as-erected shoring layout to the approved falsework drawings, and only after that field comparison was complete did the laboratory and analytical phase of the work begin. Dimensional survey of the wreckage, erection photographs and the inspector's daily reports are the ordinary materials of such a comparison.\n\nMetallurgical examination of fracture surfaces distinguished ductile overload from brittle fracture in the steel shoring members. Load path analysis occupied a later section of the report and established the sequence in which the supporting elements failed. Structural analysis recalculated the falsework capacity using the measured dimensions of the recovered members. That recalculation showed how little reserve the temporary works possessed in their as-built configuration, and on the strength of it the board of inquiry concluded that the collapse resulted from a construction deviation and not from an inadequate falsework design. The reasoning is instructive for the general case: design error and construction deviation are separated not by opinion but by the measurable gap between the geometry that was approved and the geometry that stood on the day of the pour.",
